 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Знакомое лицо в зале  (A Familiar Face in the Hall)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "sasha": "Саша"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "This is the job interview. Max walks into the dining room of Claude Monet and asks a waitress for the owner. Then he looks across the room, and everything goes wrong, because he recognises the woman running the floor. Here it is in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Здравствуйте! Я пришёл на встречу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Добрый день! Вы к Дмитрию Владимировичу?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, он приглашал меня на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хозяин пока занят на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Понятно. А кто руководит залом?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Вон та строгая девушка у окна.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Как зовут эту красивую девушку?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Это Виктория Сергеевна, наш арт-директор.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "О нет, какая неприятная ситуация!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Что случилось? Вы так побледнели!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ничего страшного, голова кружится.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хотите сесть за свободный столик?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Нет, спасибо, я лучше постою.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go through what Max and the waitress say.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Здравствуйте! Я пришёл на встречу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Hello! I arrived for an appointment.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Здравствуйте!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the formal hello, and the first v is silent, so it sounds a bit different. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на встречу.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"for a meeting\", the word you'd use for any appointment.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Добрый день! Вы к Дмитрию Владимировичу?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good day! Are you here to see Dmitry Vladimirovich?' Russians address people formally by first name plus father's name. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Владимировичу?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means \"son of Vladimir\". After \"to\", the name takes the ending -u.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, он приглашал меня на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, he invited me for a job.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "приглашал",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"invited\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на работу.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the everyday phrase for going to a job: the word for job turns its -a into -u after \"na\" when there's movement towards it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хозяин пока занят на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The owner is busy in the kitchen for now.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хозяин",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the owner, literally the master of the house. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "пока",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"for now\", exactly like the casual bye, which means \"see you for now\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Понятно. А кто руководит залом?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Understood. And who is managing the dining room?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "руководит",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"manages\", is built from hand, and to lead. It's followed by the -om form, so the hall becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "залом?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Вон та строгая девушка у окна.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That strict young woman over by the window.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Вон",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"over there\", a further point than \"here is\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "строгая",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "та",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " both end like a woman's word, because young woman is feminine. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "у окна.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"at the window\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Как зовут эту красивую девушку?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is this beautiful young woman's name?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Как зовут",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is literally \"how do they call\", with no \"they\" named. It's the normal way to ask what someone is called.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Это Виктория Сергеевна, наш арт-директор.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is Viktoria Sergeyevna, our art director.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Сергеевна,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"daughter of Sergey\", the feminine version of a father's name. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "арт-директор.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is \"art director\", borrowed whole.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "О нет, какая неприятная ситуация!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh no, what an unpleasant situation!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "неприятная",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a negative prefix plus pleasant: unpleasant. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "какая",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " at the start turns the sentence into \"what a...!\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Что случилось? Вы так побледнели!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What happened? You turned so pale!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "побледнели!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has pale inside it, and it ends in -li, the plural, even though one person is being addressed. That's how the polite you works.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ничего страшного, голова кружится.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Nothing terrible, my head is spinning.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ничего страшного,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is literally \"nothing scary\", the standard way to say \"no big deal\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "голова кружится.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has a circle in it: the head goes round.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хотите сесть за свободный столик?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Would you like to sit at an available table?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Хотите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the formal \"do you want\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "столик?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a little table, which is how a restaurant table is named. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "свободный",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is free, empty.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Нет, спасибо, я лучше постою.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, thank you, I'd rather stand.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "лучше",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", \"better\", is how Russians say \"I'd rather\". It's the polite way to turn down a seat. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
